<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-5-1-areas-distances" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Areas and Distances</title>
  <!-- Stewart 5.1 . Tue Sep 15 . Built from Handout 5 (Areas and Distances) in the
       conversational handout voice. Written 2026-09-18.
       NOTATION QUARANTINE: no integral sign anywhere in this section. It enters in 5.2.
       Keep the car example exactly as in Handout 4/5: v(t) = 3t^2, s(0) = 0, s(2) = 8.
       This section is also the direct sequel to the "Guess How Far Pancake Ran" Doenet
       pre-assignment students did before this class, same table, same L4/R4 numbers. -->

  <introduction>
    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-question-areas-distances">
      <title>Today's Question</title>
      <p>
        Can we get the change in position directly from the velocity
        &#8212; without ever finding the position function?
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <p>
      Remember our car? Velocity <m>v(t) = 3t^2</m> meters per second, starting at
      <m>s(0) = 0</m>. In <xref ref="subsec-4-9-car"/> we worked backward: we found
      an antiderivative <m>s(t) = t^3</m>, and then <m>s(2) - s(0) = 8</m> meters.
    </p>
    <p>
      That method needed a formula for <m>s</m>. Today we ask what happens if we
      only ever know the velocity, and never find <m>s</m> at all. For now, pretend
      we do not know the position function.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-1-constant-velocity">
    <title>Start with constant velocity</title>

    <p>
      Suppose a car travels at a constant <m>20</m> meters per second for
      <m>3</m> seconds. How far does it go?
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-constant-velocity-distance" label="check-constant-velocity-distance">
      <title>Easy case</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          distance <m>=</m> <fillin answer="60" mode="number"/> meters
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. <m>20 \cdot 3 = 60</m> meters.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      Now graph the velocity: a flat line at height <m>20</m>, from <m>t = 0</m> to
      <m>t = 3</m>. The region underneath it is a rectangle, with width <m>3</m>
      and height <m>20</m>. Its area is also <m>60</m>.
    </p>

    <fact xml:id="fact-distance-is-area">
      <title>Distance is area</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          When velocity is constant, distance traveled equals the area under the
          velocity graph.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </fact>

    <p>
      Why does this happen? Check the units:
      <md>
        \left(\frac{\text{meters}}{\text{second}}\right)(\text{seconds}) = \text{meters}.
      </md>
      Multiplying a rate by a length of time is exactly what finding the area of a
      rectangle does: height times width.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-1-changing-velocity">
    <title>But our car does not move at a constant velocity</title>

    <p>
      Return to <m>v(t) = 3t^2</m>. The velocity changes every instant, so we
      cannot just multiply one velocity by <m>2</m> seconds.
    </p>
    <p>
      What if we break the two seconds into smaller pieces, and pretend the
      velocity is constant on each small piece? You may recognize this table from
      your warm-up before class &#8212; dividing <m>0 \le t \le 2</m> into four equal
      pieces of width <m>\Delta t = 0.5</m> gives:
    </p>

    <tabular halign="center">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell><m>t</m></cell>
        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
        <cell><m>0.5</m></cell>
        <cell><m>1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>1.5</m></cell>
        <cell><m>2</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>v(t) = 3t^2</m></cell>
        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
        <cell><m>0.75</m></cell>
        <cell><m>3</m></cell>
        <cell><m>6.75</m></cell>
        <cell><m>12</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>

    <p>
      Over a short interval, we can pretend the velocity is approximately
      constant. From <m>t = 0.5</m> to <m>t = 1</m>, the width is
      <m>\Delta t = 0.5</m>. Using the velocity at the <em>start</em> of the
      interval, <m>v(0.5) = 0.75</m>, the change in position is approximately
      <md>
        \Delta s \approx v(0.5)\,\Delta t = (0.75)(0.5) = 0.375 \text{ meters}.
      </md>
      Geometrically, this is the area of one thin rectangle.
    </p>

    <fact xml:id="fact-rectangle-approximates-change">
      <title>One rectangle, one small change</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          rectangle area <m>=</m> (velocity)(time) <m>\approx</m> change in position.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </fact>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-1-riemann-sums">
    <title>Add the small changes together</title>

    <p>
      Use the velocity at the <em>left endpoint</em> of each of the four
      intervals <m>[0, 0.5]</m>, <m>[0.5, 1]</m>, <m>[1, 1.5]</m>, <m>[1.5, 2]</m>.
      This is called a <term>left-endpoint sum</term>, written <m>L_4</m>:
      <md>
        L_4 = v(0)(0.5) + v(0.5)(0.5) + v(1)(0.5) + v(1.5)(0.5).
      </md>
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-left-endpoint-sum" label="check-left-endpoint-sum">
      <title>Compute L4</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>L_4 =</m> <fillin answer="5.25" mode="number"/> meters
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback>
              <p>
                Yes. <m>L_4 = 0.5(0 + 0.75 + 3 + 6.75) = 0.5(10.5) = 5.25</m> meters.
                Because <m>v(t) = 3t^2</m> is increasing, each left-endpoint height is
                the <em>smallest</em> velocity on its interval, so <m>L_4</m> is an
                underestimate.
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      Now do the same thing with <em>right</em> endpoints:
      <md>
        R_4 = v(0.5)(0.5) + v(1)(0.5) + v(1.5)(0.5) + v(2)(0.5).
      </md>
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-right-endpoint-sum" label="check-right-endpoint-sum">
      <title>Compute R4</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>R_4 =</m> <fillin answer="11.25" mode="number"/> meters
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback>
              <p>
                Yes. <m>R_4 = 0.5(0.75 + 3 + 6.75 + 12) = 0.5(22.5) = 11.25</m> meters.
                This time each height is the largest velocity on its interval, so
                <m>R_4</m> is an overestimate.
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      So the true change in position is trapped:
      <md>
        5.25 \;\lt\; \text{change in position} \;\lt\; 11.25.
      </md>
    </p>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-8-is-trapped">
      <title>There it is again</title>
      <p>
        Remember <xref ref="assemblage-remember-8" text="custom">the number 8</xref>
        from our antiderivative work? It sits right between <m>5.25</m> and
        <m>11.25</m>, exactly where it should.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      Can we do better than <m>L_4</m> and <m>R_4</m>? One idea: use the
      <em>midpoint</em> of each interval instead of an endpoint.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-midpoint-sum" label="check-midpoint-sum">
      <title>Compute M4</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the midpoints <m>0.25</m>, <m>0.75</m>, <m>1.25</m>, <m>1.75</m>,
          <md>
            M_4 = 0.5\big[v(0.25) + v(0.75) + v(1.25) + v(1.75)\big].
          </md>
          <m>M_4 =</m> <fillin answer="7.875" mode="number"/> meters
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback>
              <p>
                Yes. <m>M_4 = 0.5(0.1875 + 1.6875 + 4.6875 + 9.1875) = 0.5(15.75) = 7.875</m>
                meters &#8212; noticeably closer to <m>8</m> than either <m>L_4</m> or <m>R_4</m>.
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      Drag <m>n</m> below to use more rectangles, and toggle the endpoint. Watch
      what happens to the estimate as the rectangles get thinner.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-riemann-rectangles">
      <caption>
        Left- and right-endpoint rectangles under <m>v(t) = 3t^2</m> on <m>[0, 2]</m>.
      </caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-riemann-rectangles" label="interactive-riemann-rectangles"
                   platform="jsxgraph" width="100%" aspect="4:3"
                   source="jsxgraph/riemann-rectangles.js">
        <slate xml:id="interactive-riemann-rectangles-slate" surface="jsxboard" aspect="4:3"/>
        <instructions>
          <p>
            Drag <m>n</m> from <m>4</m> up toward <m>20</m>. Toggle the checkbox to
            compare left- and right-endpoint rectangles. Where does the estimate head?
          </p>
        </instructions>
      </interactive>
    </figure>

    <exercise xml:id="notice-more-rectangles">
      <title>What did you notice?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>As <m>n</m> grows, what happens to the gap between <m>L_n</m> and <m>R_n</m>?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The gap shrinks toward <m>0</m>, and both <m>L_n</m> and <m>R_n</m> squeeze in
          on the same number: <m>8</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-1-area-problem">
    <title>The same idea, without any velocity at all</title>

    <p>
      Forget velocity for a moment. Suppose we want the area under
      <m>f(x) = x^2</m> from <m>x = 0</m> to <m>x = 1</m>. This region is not a
      rectangle, triangle, or circle, so there is no ready-made formula.
    </p>
    <p>
      We do exactly what we just did: replace one hard region with many easy
      rectangles. Divide <m>[0, 1]</m> into four equal pieces, so
      <m>\Delta x = 0.25</m>. Using left endpoints,
      <md>
        L_4 = 0.25\left[0^2 + \left(\tfrac14\right)^2 + \left(\tfrac12\right)^2 + \left(\tfrac34\right)^2\right] = \tfrac{7}{32} = 0.21875.
      </md>
      Using right endpoints,
      <md>
        R_4 = 0.25\left[\left(\tfrac14\right)^2 + \left(\tfrac12\right)^2 + \left(\tfrac34\right)^2 + 1^2\right] = \tfrac{15}{32} = 0.46875.
      </md>
      Since <m>x^2</m> is increasing on <m>[0,1]</m>, <m>L_4 &lt; \text{actual area} &lt; R_4</m>.
    </p>

    <p>Compare the two situations side by side:</p>

    <tabular halign="left">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell>Area problem</cell>
        <cell>Distance problem</cell>
      </row>
      <row><cell>width <m>= \Delta x</m></cell><cell>time width <m>= \Delta t</m></cell></row>
      <row><cell>height <m>= f(x)</m></cell><cell>height <m>= v(t)</m></cell></row>
      <row><cell><m>f(x)\Delta x</m>: small rectangle area</cell><cell><m>v(t)\Delta t</m>: small change in position</cell></row>
      <row><cell>add the rectangles</cell><cell>add the changes in position</cell></row>
    </tabular>

    <assemblage>
      <title>The same rectangle sum, two different stories</title>
      <p>
        The same rectangle sum can represent an <em>area</em> under a curve, or an
        accumulated physical quantity such as a <em>distance travelled</em>.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-1-general-recipe">
    <title>The general rectangle recipe</title>

    <p>
      Suppose <m>f</m> is positive on <m>[a, b]</m>. Break the interval into
      <m>n</m> equal pieces, each of width
      <md>
        \Delta x = \frac{b - a}{n}.
      </md>
      Inside each small interval, choose one point <m>x_i^*</m>. One rectangle has
      area <m>f(x_i^*)\,\Delta x</m>. Adding all <m>n</m> rectangles gives
      <md>
        f(x_1^*)\Delta x + f(x_2^*)\Delta x + \cdots + f(x_n^*)\Delta x.
      </md>
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-riemann-sum">
      <title>Riemann sum</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A sum of the form
          <md>
            \sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i^*)\,\Delta x
          </md>
          is called a <term>Riemann sum</term> for <m>f</m> on <m>[a, b]</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      What happens as we use more and more, thinner and thinner rectangles?
      As <m>n \to \infty</m>, we have <m>\Delta x \to 0</m>, and our approximation
      approaches the exact area:
      <md>
        A = \lim_{n \to \infty} \sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i^*)\,\Delta x.
      </md>
    </p>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Important idea</title>
      <p>
        We do not build rectangles because rectangles are the goal. We build
        rectangles because their areas are easy to compute, and increasingly thin
        rectangles let us capture a curved region exactly.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <remark xml:id="remark-integral-notation-coming">
      <title>A note on notation</title>
      <p>
        That limit expression is correct, but long. Next class we give it a much
        shorter name &#8212; and along with it, a symbol you may already have seen
        somewhere. We are saving it for <xref ref="sec-5-2-definite-integral"/> on
        purpose, so you see exactly where it comes from first.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-1-table-examples">
    <title>When all we have is a table</title>

    <p>
      A cyclist's velocity is recorded every two seconds:
    </p>

    <tabular halign="center">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell><m>t</m> (s)</cell>
        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
        <cell><m>2</m></cell>
        <cell><m>4</m></cell>
        <cell><m>6</m></cell>
        <cell><m>8</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>v(t)</m> (m/s)</cell>
        <cell><m>2</m></cell>
        <cell><m>4</m></cell>
        <cell><m>7</m></cell>
        <cell><m>9</m></cell>
        <cell><m>10</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>

    <p>We have no formula for <m>v(t)</m>, but we can still estimate the distance.</p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-cyclist-table" label="check-cyclist-table">
      <title>Estimate from a table</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Each interval has width <m>\Delta t = 2</m> seconds.
          <m>L_4 =</m> <fillin answer="44" mode="number"/> meters, and
          <m>R_4 =</m> <fillin answer="60" mode="number"/> meters.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. <m>L_4 = 2(2 + 4 + 7 + 9) = 44</m> meters.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. <m>R_4 = 2(4 + 7 + 9 + 10) = 60</m> meters.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      Now suppose oil leaks from a tank at a <em>decreasing</em> rate:
    </p>

    <tabular halign="center">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell><m>t</m> (hours)</cell>
        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
        <cell><m>2</m></cell>
        <cell><m>4</m></cell>
        <cell><m>6</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>r(t)</m> (liters/hour)</cell>
        <cell><m>10</m></cell>
        <cell><m>8</m></cell>
        <cell><m>7</m></cell>
        <cell><m>5</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>

    <exercise xml:id="check-oil-leak-table" label="check-oil-leak-table">
      <title>A decreasing rate</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the rate at the <em>beginning</em> of each two-hour interval,
          amount <m>\approx (10)(2) + (8)(2) + (7)(2) =</m>
          <fillin answer="50" mode="number"/> liters.
        </p>
        <p>Since the rate is decreasing, is this an overestimate or an underestimate?</p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback>
              <p>
                Yes, <m>50</m> liters. And because the rate is <em>decreasing</em>, using
                the rate at the beginning of each interval means we always use the
                <em>largest</em> rate on that interval &#8212; so this is an overestimate.
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <fact xml:id="fact-unit-test">
      <title>The unit test</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          One of the fastest ways to understand an accumulation problem is to
          multiply the units: rate <m>\times</m> small input change <m>=</m> small
          accumulated amount. This works whether the rate is
          <m>\text{meters}/\text{second}</m>, <m>\text{liters}/\text{minute}</m>, or
          <m>\text{dollars}/\text{item}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </fact>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-5-1-practice">
    <title>Putting it together</title>

    <exercise xml:id="try-right-endpoint-sum">
      <title>You do: a right-endpoint estimate</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Estimate the area under <m>f(x) = x^2 + 1</m> from <m>x = 0</m> to
          <m>x = 2</m> using four right-endpoint rectangles.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>\Delta x = \tfrac{2 - 0}{4} = 0.5</m>, with right endpoints
          <m>0.5, 1, 1.5, 2</m>.
          <md>
            R_4 = 0.5\big[f(0.5) + f(1) + f(1.5) + f(2)\big] = 0.5(1.25 + 2 + 3.25 + 5) = 5.75.
          </md>
          Since <m>f</m> is increasing on <m>[0, 2]</m>, this is an overestimate.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="check-conceptual-areas-distances">
      <title>Conceptual check</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Why does a rectangle under a velocity graph represent a distance?</p></li>
            <li><p>What does making the rectangles thinner accomplish?</p></li>
            <li><p>For an increasing function, which gives the larger estimate: left endpoints or right endpoints?</p></li>
            <li><p>If a quantity changes at rate <m>r(t)</m>, what does <m>r(t)\Delta t</m> approximately represent? What does <em>adding</em> many such terms represent?</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Multiplying velocity by a time width gives (rate)(time) <m>=</m> distance, which is exactly a rectangle's area.</p></li>
            <li><p>Thinner rectangles hug the curve more closely, so the approximation gets more accurate.</p></li>
            <li><p>Right endpoints give the larger estimate, since the function's largest value on each interval sits at the right edge.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>r(t)\Delta t</m> approximates one small accumulated amount. Adding many of them approximates the total accumulated amount.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exit-ticket-areas-distances">
      <title>Exit ticket</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>A car travels at <m>30</m> ft/s for <m>4</m> seconds. How far does it travel?</p></li>
            <li><p>A velocity graph is divided into rectangles of width <m>0.2</m> seconds. One rectangle has height <m>15</m> ft/s. What physical quantity does <m>(15)(0.2)</m> represent?</p></li>
            <li><p>If a positive function is increasing, is a left-endpoint sum usually an underestimate or an overestimate?</p></li>
            <li><p>In your own words, why can rectangles help us find the area under a curve?</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p><m>30 \cdot 4 = 120</m> feet.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>3</m> feet &#8212; the distance travelled during that <m>0.2</m>-second interval.</p></li>
            <li><p>An underestimate, since the left edge holds the smallest value on an increasing function.</p></li>
            <li><p>Because a curved region can be approximated as closely as we like by many thin rectangles, whose areas we already know how to compute.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Today's main idea</title>
      <p>
        A changing quantity can be accumulated by breaking time or space into small
        pieces, approximating each piece with a rectangle, and adding:
        rate <m>\times</m> small interval <m>\approx</m> small accumulated amount.
      </p>
      <p>
        One method works backward from the rate, using an antiderivative. The other
        method adds up the rate, using increasingly thin rectangles. Soon we will
        discover why these two methods give exactly the same answer.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>
</section>
